@description ReaSock: connect REAPER to other programs
@version 0.3.0
@author Tech Audio
@links
  GitHub https://github.com/TeamAudio/reasock
  Tech Audio https://techaud.io
@about
  # ReaSock
  
  A bridge between REAPER and other programs. ReaSock opens a local WebSocket
  that speaks JSON-RPC 2.0, so tools outside REAPER can:
  
  - **Lend** REAPER actions: register entries in the action list that users can
    bind to keys, menus, and toolbars, and get called when they run
  - **Tell**: follow transport, project, and selection changes as they happen
  - **Ask**: read tracks, items, takes, and markers
  
  Scripts inside REAPER can use the same protocol through ReaSock_Open,
  ReaSock_Call, and ReaSock_Poll.
  
  **ReaSock_Inspector panel** (needs ReaImGui) shows the project, transport,
  and selection live, as an example of using ReaSock from a script.
  
  Run **ReaSock: Open API documentation** from the Actions list, or call
  `reaper.ReaSock_OpenDocs()`, for the full reference.
@changelog
  - Protocol 0.4.0: ReaSock can now change the project, so a sound library or media browser can place sounds in REAPER.
  - `items.insert` places media files, or parts of them, at the edit cursor or a given time: on the current track, a given one, or a new one; one after another, each on its own track, or as takes of one item. Each file can bring a take name, item notes, and extension data. Every file is checked first, so a request with a bad file changes nothing, and the error says which file and why.
  - `items.set` and `takes.set` change item notes, take names, and extension data (per-item and per-take strings saved in the project), in batches.
  - Every change is one undo point, named after the client unless it passes its own name.
  - Peaks for inserted files are built a little at a time, so long files don't stall REAPER.
  - `items.list`, `items.listSelected`, and `items.get` can include item notes and extension data; `takes.list` can include extension data.
  - Items now report their active take (`takeGuid`, `startOffset`, `playRate`) and whether they loop their source, so a client can work out which part of a file an item plays. Slip edits now count as changes in `items.selectionChanged`.
  - `transport.get` and `transport.changed` include the edit cursor and the time selection.
  - `initialize` lists the methods the server supports (`capabilities.methods`), so clients can tell what an older ReaSock lacks.
  - `debug.stats` reports main-thread time per method.
  - `@techaudio/reasock`: `createClient()` returns a client right away and connects when REAPER starts, for apps that start before REAPER. Actions and subscriptions set up meanwhile wait for the connection. Calls fail at once with `NotConnectedError` while disconnected, and a REAPER that doesn't answer in time gives `TimeoutError`, so apps can tell "not running" from "busy". `supports()` says whether the connected ReaSock has a method, `RpcError` carries the error's `data`, and the `signal` option stops a client.
  - Examples: `place.mjs` places media files at the edit cursor.
